# {{ appKey }} App

## Start with this App

- Read `nexia.json`, `composer.json`, `package.json`, `README.md`, `docs/README.md`,
  and relevant App-owned docs for identity, commands, and domain rules. Inspect
  the affected source and callers before editing; preserve existing work,
  migrations, and data.
- Work only in this App using installed public SDK contracts. Do not import,
  copy, or patch Core, another App, or installed dependencies. Report a missing
  public capability through developer support without exposing credentials or data.
- Check the installed SDK versions and target host compatibility before using a
  contract. Read only the relevant guides below; use `nexia docs search "topic"`
  and `nexia docs read <document-id>` or the linked website when needed.

## Choose guidance for the task

| Task | Read before implementation |
| --- | --- |
| Frontend screens and CSS | https://developers.nexia.to/docs/building-apps-build-a-frontend-surface and https://developers.nexia.to/docs/app-sdk-frontend-host-contracts and see https://developers.nexia.to/examples |
| Resource, data model and migration | https://developers.nexia.to/docs/building-apps-create-a-resource and https://developers.nexia.to/docs/building-apps-data-model-and-migrations |
| API, actor and authorization | https://developers.nexia.to/docs/building-apps-add-api-and-authorization |
| Cross-App data and batching | https://developers.nexia.to/docs/platform-extensions-resource-references |
| Events and background work | https://developers.nexia.to/docs/building-apps-handle-events-and-async-work |
| Translation catalogs | https://developers.nexia.to/docs/building-apps-localize-an-app |
| Testing and release | https://developers.nexia.to/docs/building-apps-test-an-app and https://developers.nexia.to/docs/building-apps-release-an-app |

For frontend work, prefer existing public `@nexia/sdk/host` components and
patterns whenever they meet the need. Use their supported props and slots;
scope custom styles to App-owned markup instead of overriding host internals.
For protected cross-App ID reads, use the existing `includeProtectedValues` context;
the owner must authorize the purpose and scope. Never log protected values or
retry without protection. Matching SDK, Core and runtime support is required.

For cross-App list references, batch unique IDs for the authorized page and
preserve actor, organization, date, and purpose boundaries. Follow the guide's
compatibility and fallback rules; do not retry omitted IDs as an access bypass.
For new cross-App, Party, user and person-profile reads, prefer the standard
chainable APIs on supported hosts: use `ResourceReferenceQueries` for cross-App
reads and `IdentityQueries` for Party, user and person-profile reads. See
https://developers.nexia.to/docs/platform-extensions-resource-references for
examples and supported conditions. A query executes at `get()`, and the result
contains `status`, `data`, `page`, and `error`; check each status before using
data. Prefer `queryBatch()` when independent queries can share a batch. Legacy
lookup methods are deprecated where an equivalent standard query is available,
but remain supported for the transition and older hosts. Preserve their return
and failure handling when maintaining old code. Pagination and historical reads
require provider support; never broaden authority to retry omitted IDs.
Existing context filters may be ignored by legacy providers; if migration
requires `allowLegacyContextFilters()`, never treat those filters as an
authorization boundary. New builder conditions still require schema validation.
For protected cross-App ID reads, use standard `query()` with an
`includeProtectedValues` context; the owner must authorize the purpose and
scope. Existing `resolve()`/`resolveMany()` also support this option on matching hosts.
Never log protected values or retry without protection. Matching SDK, Core and
runtime support is required.
For translations, reuse supported host `common.*` keys for shared UI and keep
App-owned domain wording in flat `resources/lang/<locale>.json` catalogs. Each
value may contain at most 2,000 Unicode characters, including descriptions.
The exported runtime catalog is limited to 1 MiB, 20 locales and 1,000 exported
keys per locale; use `nexia check` to locate translation violations. Fix
validation errors, including `CATALOG_VALIDATION_FAILED`, before retrying;
never delete `.nexia` or reset data to bypass recovery status.

## App-owned guidance and delivery

Maintain this file with the App's domain constraints, conventions, and focused
checks. Keep shared platform details in the linked guides. Existing App files
and instructions belong to their developer and are not replaced by tool updates.

Use the checks in README.md. At validation, start with relevant `nexia check`,
`nexia test`, or `nexia test --frontend`, then verify changed behavior and
permissions in the sandbox. Report source checks, observed runtime behavior and
unverified items separately. Draft changes defer automated checks until testing,
verification, finalization, commit, push, or PR is requested. Use a purpose-named
branch. Never commit, push, submit,
merge, publish, reset data, or send messages without authorization.
